            "In 1978 the nations of the world met at Alma-Ata and defined <strong>primary health care</strong> as essential health care, based on practical, scientifically sound and socially acceptable methods and technology, made universally accessible to individuals and families in the community through their full participation, at a cost the community and country can afford, forming the first level of contact with the national health system and bringing care as close as possible to where people live and work. Every phrase is examinable, but the sentence has one meaning: care goes to the people, not the people to the hospital. The declaration was renewed at Astana in 2018 as the route to <strong>universal health coverage</strong> — everyone receiving the services they need without financial hardship.",
            "Four <strong>principles</strong> run through PHC. <strong>Equitable distribution</strong>: services reach the hamlet 12 km from the road, not only the town on it. <strong>Community participation</strong>: people take part in planning, running and evaluating their own services rather than receiving them. <strong>Intersectoral collaboration</strong>: health is made by agriculture, education, water, sanitation, roads and local government as much as by clinics, so the health team works with all of them. <strong>Appropriate technology</strong>: methods that are effective, affordable, acceptable and maintainable where they are used — the MUAC tape, ORS, the malaria rapid test, the solar fridge, the motorbike or tricycle ambulance, the drone that drops blood and vaccines, the mobile phone that sends the weekly report. A fifth idea, the emphasis on <strong>prevention and health promotion</strong> over cure, holds the others together.",
