            "Ghana adopted PHC in 1978 with a three-tier district system — the community (level A, with village health workers and trained TBAs), the sub-district health centre (level B) and the district hospital and health management team (level C) — and in the Bamako Initiative of 1987 added community co-financing through revolving drug funds. Coverage improved in towns and stalled in the villages, because the system still waited for people to walk to it. The <strong>Navrongo Community Health and Family Planning Project</strong>, begun in 1994 in Kassena-Nankana, tested a simple reversal: relocate a trained nurse to live in the community, give her a motorbike and essential drugs, support her with volunteers chosen by the community and with the traditional leadership, and let her go house to house. Child mortality and fertility fell where the nurse lived among the people and changed little where only volunteers or only clinics were used. In 1999 the government made the model national policy as <strong>Community-based Health Planning and Services</strong>; the revised National CHPS Policy of 2016 placed CHPS at the centre of Ghana's route to universal health coverage, and there are now more than 6,000 functional CHPS zones.",
