            "Many Ghanaian communities carry the scars of programmes that worked until the funding ended: the latrines built by an NGO and never used, the volunteers who stopped when the allowances stopped, the compound without a nurse. <strong>Sustainability</strong> is designed at the start, not hoped for at the end. It rests on <strong>community ownership</strong> — the CHMC, not the district, owns the compound and the programme; on <strong>integration</strong> into the routine Ghana Health Service structure and budget rather than a parallel project; on <strong>local resources</strong> — the community's land, labour, levy or farm plot, the district assembly's common fund; on <strong>appropriate technology</strong> that the community can maintain; on <strong>training local people</strong> rather than importing skills; and on <strong>realistic incentives</strong> for volunteers — recognition at the durbar, NHIS registration, bicycles, T-shirts, priority at the clinic, a small farm the community works for them — agreed openly so that no one feels deceived when there is no salary.",
